#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088478} :activity/id #uuid "db2aa40b-702a-52ed-baea-d499f50ff54a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088880 :tutorial/content "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x+y) = \\cos x \\cos y - \\sin x \\sin y\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $y={\\color{blue}x}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\cos 2x = \\cos^2 {x}  - \\sin^2 {x}.\n$$\n\nThis is called the **double-angle formula for cosine**." :tutorial/id #uuid "8597693a-d6bd-59b2-bc3a-6922057a55be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2638 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "509b8966-516b-5a1a-850b-bc2e1fe1df97" :step/math-academy-id 12374 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477 :question/id #uuid "7826cd52-8603-51ff-902e-c7764639dccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3666" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)} - \\sin^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ec37c5e-8a40-58a7-8af3-4457cd5da43f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749147} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186749140}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "9a6deb20-15ba-561d-abcd-d2927bc14f03" :step/math-academy-id 12391 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088483}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088881 :tutorial/content "It's possible to write the double angle formula for cosine in two alternative forms.\n\nFrom the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2{x}+\\cos^2{x}=1,$ we obtain $\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}.$\nSubstituting this into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =(1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives an alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine. We'll restate it below:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =1 - 2\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, from the same Pythagorean identity, we obtain that $\\sin^2{x}=1-\\cos^2{x}.$ Substituting into the double-angle formula for cosine, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-{\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡x-(1-{\\cos }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is another alternative form of the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} =2\\cos^2{x}-1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5da93916-34c4-5c5a-806c-82bae4dc1e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2639 :tutorial/title "Alternative Forms of the Double-Angle Formula for Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "2058340e-7371-58c9-98d0-c006925b2662" :step/math-academy-id 12394 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087478 :question/id #uuid "2b9dc462-ec26-5762-aa64-d9e8f16112c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3667" :question/problem "Express $2\\cos^2{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}-1$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n2 \\cos^2 x - 1 = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703575} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186598566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186699008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703570}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "e9090058-9d48-50de-beb0-3295dfa48fcc" :step/math-academy-id 12395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087479 :question/id #uuid "e617aaf2-1232-57cc-8644-2467effda030" :question/math-academy-id "e-3668" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\cos^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right) - \\sin^2\\left( 22.5^\\circ\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={22.5^\\circ}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘})-{\\sin }^{2}⁡({22.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {22.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2902b205-4508-55c6-9a68-f57d3afc9eab" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218